<details class="tk-cj-event" open><summary><span class="tk-cj-index">03</span><span class="tk-cj-event-main"><strong>First purchase</strong><small>Order #10482 · $129.00</small></span><span class="tk-cj-type">Commerce</span><span class="tk-cj-chevron" aria-hidden="true">⌄</span></summary><div class="tk-cj-event-detail"><b>Illustrative source evidence</b><p>Exact merchant and order references help establish commerce identity.</p></div></details>
<details class="tk-cj-event" ><summary><span class="tk-cj-index">04</span><span class="tk-cj-event-main"><strong>Post-purchase upsell</strong><small>Additional order · $49.00</small></span><span class="tk-cj-type">Commerce</span><span class="tk-cj-chevron" aria-hidden="true">⌄</span></summary><div class="tk-cj-event-detail"><b>Illustrative source evidence</b><p>Upsells remain distinct transactions connected through source relationships.</p></div></details>
<details class="tk-cj-event" ><summary><span class="tk-cj-index">05</span><span class="tk-cj-event-main"><strong>Subscription rebill</strong><small>Recurring charge · $69.00</small></span><span class="tk-cj-type">Lifecycle</span><span class="tk-cj-chevron" aria-hidden="true">⌄</span></summary><div class="tk-cj-event-detail"><b>Illustrative source evidence</b><p>Rebills can be related to the original customer and subscription when identifiers support the link.</p></div></details>
<details class="tk-cj-event" ><summary><span class="tk-cj-index">06</span><span class="tk-cj-event-main"><strong>Partial refund</strong><small>Refund · −$35.00</small></span><span class="tk-cj-type">Adjustment</span><span class="tk-cj-chevron" aria-hidden="true">⌄</span></summary><div class="tk-cj-event-detail"><b>Illustrative source evidence</b><p>Financial adjustments remain separate evidence with their own source references.</p></div></details>
<details class="tk-cj-event" ><summary><span class="tk-cj-index">07</span><span class="tk-cj-event-main"><strong>Chargeback review</strong><small>Dispute · Pending</small></span><span class="tk-cj-type">Dispute</span><span class="tk-cj-chevron" aria-hidden="true">⌄</span></summary><div class="tk-cj-event-detail"><b>Illustrative source evidence</b><p>A dispute can be investigated without assuming its attribution or final financial outcome.</p></div></details></div><p class="tk-cj-demo-note">Figures are illustrative. Net collected is not net profit; advertising, processing, fulfillment, dispute outcomes, and other costs are not included.</p></div>
<div class="tk-cj-section-head"><span class="tk-ip-kicker">THE EVIDENCE BEHIND THE JOURNEY</span><h2>Connections you can explain.</h2><p>TraceKit distinguishes the commercial transaction from its acquisition attribution, keeping original source evidence intact wherever possible.</p></div>
<div class="tk-cj-benefits"><article><span>01</span><h3>Preserve both identities.</h3><p>Understand which commercial transaction an event belongs to and where the acquisition originated—without collapsing both into one generic ID.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Connect with confidence.</h3><p>Prioritize exact provider, order, transaction, and shared identifiers over uncertain customer matching.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Keep every event distinct.</h3><p>Base orders, upsells, rebills, refunds, disputes, and adjustments remain their own source and financial events.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Show what's unresolved.</h3><p>Missing identifiers shouldn't make a record disappear. Keep unmatched evidence visible for investigation.</p></article></div>
<div class="tk-cj-outcome"><div><span class="tk-ip-kicker">BEYOND THE FIRST CONVERSION</span><h2>Understand more than revenue.</h2><p>Follow the relationship between marketing acquisition, customer purchases, and subsequent financial events. Investigate discrepancies and see where additional source data is needed before drawing a conclusion.</p><small>Available journeys and financial views depend on the integrations and evidence supported for your account.</small></div><div class="tk-cj-outcome-stack"><div><span>Acquisition</span><strong>Where did the customer come from?</strong></div><div><span>Commerce</span><strong>What did they purchase next?</strong></div><div><span>Financial</span><strong>What changed after the sale?</strong></div></div></div>
